From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zedex <zedex@invalid>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 10:06:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Turbo: a 7MHz Z80 on the clones that had one

The Pentagons and the Scorpion ran their Z80 at twice 3.5MHz while keeping the
50Hz frame and everything else about the display, so a game got twice the work
done between two interrupts and its music played at the same tempo.  That is
not settings_current.emulation_speed, which makes the whole machine - music,
tape and real time with it - run faster; it is twice as many tstates inside a
frame that still lasts a fiftieth of a second.

So every field of machine_timings is multiplied together, and a frame still
takes tstates_per_frame / processor_speed seconds.  The borders and the line
length are in that list because the display is driven from them: leave those
alone and the picture would be drawn in the first half of every frame.

Three things do not follow the CPU, and each was a bug before it was a line:

  - The AY has a clock of its own and stays at 1.75MHz, so sound.c multiplies
    AY_CLOCK_RATIO by machine_turbo_factor().  Without it every tune plays an
    octave up in turbo, which is the first thing this got wrong.
  - ULA_CONTENTION_SIZE was 80000, one entry per tstate, and the Pentagon's
    doubled frame is 143360.  The array is indexed by a running tstate count
    with no bound check of its own, so it is 160000 now.
  - The contention array is filled again when the frame changes length, even
    though every machine with a turbo is uncontended and it fills with zeroes:
    entries past the old frame length would otherwise be read on the very
    first turbo frame.

machine_set_turbo() is for changing it while the machine runs, and has to be
called at the end of a frame - which is where the UI's events are drained, so
tstates is back to nearly nothing.  It re-schedules the frame's own event,
since spectrum_frame() has already booked the next one at the old length:
leaving it there costs one frame at the wrong speed turning turbo on, and one
that lasts twice as long - a visible stutter - turning it off.  The caller
restarts the sound, because sound_init() reads the processor speed once, for
the blip buffer's clock rate.

settings_current.turbo is the only copy of whether it is on, so that the
command line - read before there is a machine to tell - and a switch in the UI
cannot disagree.  settings.c and settings.h are regenerated from settings.dat
here for the same reason patch 0002 carried them.
